screen" width=3D"526"></iframe></div><br><p style=3D"text-align: center;"><=
span style=3D"font-size: x-small;">RedHat 7.2 (GNOME 1.4) with 12MB RAM and=
 1MB VRAM</span></p><p>Thus I can see how reasonable it is to require somet=
hing like 32MB RAM. That lag wouldn't happen if one is multitasking. So usi=
ng this logic, I realized (many years ago), that I could optimize a RedHat-=
like distro to run just Fluxbox or FLTK/FLWM, or xfce (which was released i=
n 1997) to run Abiword as a single application. Gnumeric could load with ju=
st 10MB of RAM, which is also a lightweight spreadsheet. That said, Abiword=
 itself uses <a href=3D"https://www.linuxjournal.com/article/6169">less tha=
n 2MB</a> of RAM. Therefore X server is the largest component of the memory=
 usage when 8 or 12MB of RAM is loaded. Therefore if I can reduce the size =
of the ISO to 8MB, then I could potentially lower the power consumption of =
the entire system by 10-20% (and far more if one compares 2000-era DRAM to =
HBM3e RAM (600nm&gt; 2nm GAA). Wondering how I am getting to this figure? T=
his <a href=3D"https://semiengineering.com/the-next-big-thing/">article</a>=
, and my <a href=3D"https://inavoyage.blogspot.com/2026/01/the-nm10-chipset=
-backporting-to-pentium.html">January article</a>, "The NM10 Chipset, Backp=
orting to the Pentium Era, and Makimoto's Wave" will fill you in. It's all =
about the energy consumption of a 1mm^2-4mm^2 18NA chip produced using EUV =
by one of ASML's latest lithography machines. The idea is if you can produc=
e a small enough chip with enough embedded DRAM, you could sell enough chip=
s per wafer- 52,000 or so at a price such as $2-4, and therefore competitiv=
e with Apple or Nvidia which reserves the same $45,000 2nm wafers for large=
 GB300 chips and where yields might be 89 per 300mm wafer, thus sold for da=
tacenters at hundreds of thousands of dollars each to recoup the cost. Then=
&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://github.com/hatonthecat/Pokey-Linux">each OS could =
run as a single app</a> as a multi boot from a microSD/USB, or wherever the=
 HDD/SSD stores the liveISO. Thus instead of having one 400MB ISO with tens=
 of apps one live ISO, tens or <i>hundreds </i>of of RedHat 7.2 ISOs would =
be stored on the microSD card at just 20-30MB in size each, one for each ap=
p, such as Gnumeric and Abiword. How would one person develop that?</p>Sang=
er's rule applied to AI objectively suggests...<span style=3D"font-family: =
inherit;">&nbsp;a</span><span style=3D"background-color: white; font-family=
: inherit;">&nbsp;discovery may be possible in absence of the funding for t=
hree employees who could do the same.</span><div><br><p style=3D"background=
-color: white; color: #222222; font-size: small; margin-bottom: 0.17in;"><s=
pan style=3D"font-family: inherit;"><span style=3D"color: black;"></span></=
span></p><blockquote><p style=3D"background-color: white; color: #222222; f=
ont-size: small; margin-bottom: 0.17in;"><span style=3D"font-family: inheri=
t;"><span style=3D"color: black;">=E2=80=9C</span><a data-saferedirecturl=
